// expect-exit: 0
// stress: 40
// explore: 1000
//
// THE LEGAL HALF OF DEF-359 + DEF-360 + DEF-361 + DEF-362 (landing 169): the trait-qualified spawn with plain
// data by value and a `move string` argument (f4d -- CLEAN on landing 167, REFUSED at `move(s2)` by landing
// 168's shifted reading, clean again here), its method spelling (f4e), an owner MOVED through the trait-
// qualified spelling into a `move Self:self`; a by-value plain-data receiver spelled `(<-pp).lookp()` -- the
// EXPLICIT dereference, which copies the pointee at the spawn (the auto-dereferenced `pp.lookp()` hands the
// task the pointer's words, DEF-363, and is refused) -- and copied into a local first; a by-value method AWAITED
// through the EXPLICIT dereference, reporting the value it read (the auto-dereferenced `await pp.peekk()` is
// DEF-363's AWAIT face -- the same child-frame builder -- and reads an address, 114 at both legs on 168 and
// 169; refused by nothing yet, so the legal half does not spell it); a generic `T` MOVED into a `move T:x`; a pick
// expression as the receiver and inside a sanctioned argument whose arm views `d` and writes ANOTHER string,
// and the root written AFTER the block's join. Each control fails at its own exit [true of every control only
// from landing 172 on: see its paragraph below]; exit 0 at both legs under
// this compiler; landing 168's checker refuses control 1 alone (the over-refusal it introduced) and accepts
// every other. The refusals of the same shapes are tests/analysis/rejection/spawn_review_faces.npk.
// LANDING 170 (2026-10-30): the child-frame seed loads the pointee (DEF-363) and a by-value receiver through a
// pointer crosses a spawn as a copy of the pointee, plain data legal (DEF-360 relaxed): controls 21-26 spell the
// AUTO-DEREFERENCE the paragraph above calls refused -- the spawn with the owner writing, with nothing written,
// through an inline address (landing 169's refusal sites 14-16), the await reporting what it read, a plain `async`
// spawn, and an owning pointee awaited by a by-value receiver; control 27 awaits a pointee whose type HOLDS AN
// ADDRESS, `H{ p: @d }`, the same way, reporting the byte it read (its spawn is the rejection file's sites 14-16).
// Landing 169's checker refuses controls 21, 22, 23 and 25 (DEF-360's blanket rule), so this file's 169 leg cannot
// run; its 168 leg could not either (control 1). Controls 28 and 29 spell the owning pointee's two transfers.
// CORRECTED 2026-10-30 (landing 171, test item C; management_7 04:21): "each control fails at its own exit" above
// was not true of a control whose TASK checks what it read -- 1-5, 7-9, 21-23, 25 and 28 failed inside the task
// (`fail E1`), the join relaying it as 114 -- and control 29 compared a LENGTH, 3 for the clone and the rewrite
// alike. Controls 21-23, 25 and 29 now have the task REPORT what it read on `done` (`lookpd`, `lookqd`, `takecd`)
// and `main` compare at the control's own exit, 29 the byte; 1-5, 7-9 and 28 keep their check inside the task [until
// landing 172: see below].
// LANDING 172 (2026-10-30): DEF-364 refuses a by-value receiver that holds a borrow, DEF-365 a type that holds a type
// parameter by value (tests/analysis/rejection/spawn_review_faces.npk sites 17-26). Controls 30-34 hold what stays
// legal: the task handed the VALUE it reads, a plain struct built from `d`'s byte, while the owner rewrites `d` (30,
// DEF-364's remedy); a receiver HOLDING a borrow AWAITED by name, the caller suspended (31; 27 is the same through a
// pointer); a generic struct's CONCRETE instance of plain data by value (32); a `T?` and a `T[2]` at `string` MOVED into
// the task's `move` parameter (33, 34). Controls 35-37 are the ARGUMENT spellings of controls 7, 8 and 28 -- the pick
// expression handed to a by-value `W` parameter, its arm viewing `d` and writing another string or `d` written after
// the join, and the owning pointee transferred into a `move SC` parameter -- which landing 171's checker refuses
// (NITPICK-BORROW-004: the walk answered YES for the byte read through the live view and for the moved pointee,
// whatever the value's type, DEF-378) and this one admits (wt/51a/.internal/probes/t172/twins/ c7a, c8a, c28a). And
// management's review of 224d907 (its F4): line 15's claim was qualified by the paragraph above, not made true -- now
// controls 1-5, 7-9 and 28 have the task report what it read on `done` (`m3`, `mm`, `lookpd`, `lookd`, `fillerd`,
// `takecd`) and `main` compare at the control's own exit, so EVERY control fails at its own exit; the owner's write
// under the task in controls 1 and 2 writes a DIFFERENT value now, 2, so the report tells the copy from the owner.
// Exit 0 at both legs under this compiler; landing 171's checker refuses controls 35, 36 and 37.
// R1'S COMMIT (landing 172, after management's review of its first build, 99f83e7): a dereference of a pointer answers
// YES in a function that LENDS -- some call, a builtin's included, handed a borrow of the frame -- since a callee's
// store the store matrix does not attribute to the target (DEF-390), an atomic's `store` and `mcpy` leave no mark the
// first build read. `main` lends, so controls 28 and 37, the pointee transferred through the pointer as a receiver and
// as an argument, are refused there (NITPICK-BORROW-004) and stand in functions of their own that lend nothing,
// `pointee_moved_recv` and `pointee_moved_arg`; `main` compares what each reports at the control's own exit. Control
// 29's clone stays in `main`, legal there. The rule is a stopgap until DEF-390's fix narrows it to the stores no
// matrix attributes.
mod:spawn_review_faces_ok;
error:E1;
fixed Duration:J2 = Duration{ ns: 2000000000i64 };
struct:W = { int64:k; };
struct:SS = { string:s; int64:k; };
struct:PD = { int64:k; };
struct:H = { string->:p; };
impl:W = {
    // Landing 172 (F4): controls 7 and 8 spawn this, and the task REPORTS what it read.
    thread async func:lookd = NIL(W:self, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) joins J2 {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        await done.send(self.k, raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        pass NIL;
    };
};
impl:PD = {
    async func:peekk = int64(PD:self) {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        pass self.k;
    };
    // Landing 171 (test item C): controls 21-23 and 25 spawn these, and the task REPORTS what it read; landing 172
    // (F4): controls 4 and 5 spawn `lookpd` too.
    thread async func:lookpd = NIL(PD:self, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) joins J2 {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        await done.send(self.k, raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        pass NIL;
    };
    async func:lookqd = NIL(PD:self, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        await done.send(self.k, raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        pass NIL;
    };
};
// Landing 172 (F4): control 9 spawns this, and the task REPORTS the value it read back.
thread async func:fillerd = NIL(shared_arena<int64>->:s, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) joins J2 {
    await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
    Handle<int64>:h = s.alloc(424242i64);
    int64:v = s.get(h) ?! E1;
    await done.send(v, raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
    pass NIL;
};
thread async func:takeg<T> = NIL(move T:x) joins J2 { await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL; pass NIL; };
// Landing 172 (F4): the tasks of controls 1-3 REPORT what they read -- `m3` its copy's `k` and the string's length,
// 100 * k + len (103), `mm` the moved string's first byte and `n`, 100 * byte + n (9705); a send takes the channel's
// level, so each trait declares `acquires <= 3i32` (NITPICK-LOCK-002 otherwise).
trait:Tr3 = { async func:m3 = NIL(Self:self, move string:n, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) acquires <= 3i32; };
impl:PD:Tr3 = {
    thread async func:m3 = NIL(PD:self, move string:n, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) joins J2 {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        await done.send(self.k * 100i64 + n.len, raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        pass NIL;
    };
};
trait:Tm = { async func:mm = NIL(move Self:self, move int64:n, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) acquires <= 3i32; };
impl:SS:Tm = {
    thread async func:mm = NIL(move SS:self, move int64:n, Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done) joins J2 {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        fixed uint8[]:xb = string_bytes(self.s);
        await done.send((xb[0i64] => int64) * 100i64 + n, raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        pass NIL;
    };
};
impl:SS = {
    async func:slen = int64(SS:self) {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        pass self.s.len;
    };
};
impl:H = {
    async func:rdb = uint8(H:self) {
        await sleep(raw duration_ms(1i64)) ?| NIL;
        fixed uint8[]:xb = string_bytes(<-self.p);
        pass xb[0i64];
    };
};
async func:generic_moved<T> = NIL(move T:a) {
    {
        drop takeg::<T>(move(a));
    }
    pass NIL;
};
async func:main = int32(cstring[]:_~argv) {
    Channel<int64, 3i32, 64i64>:done = channel() ?! E1;
    // 1. (f4d) the trait-qualified spawn: plain data by value as `Self:self`, a `move string` argument
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        string:s2 = string_concat("ab", "c");
        {
            drop Tr3.m3(pd, move(s2), done);
            pd = PD{ k: 2i64 };
        }
        int64:got1 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got1 != 103i64) { exit 11i32; }
        if (pd.k != 2i64) { exit 11i32; }
    }
    // 2. (f4e) the same spawn spelled as a method call
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        string:s2 = string_concat("ab", "c");
        {
            drop pd.m3(move(s2), done);
            pd = PD{ k: 2i64 };
        }
        int64:got2 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got2 != 103i64) { exit 12i32; }
        if (pd.k != 2i64) { exit 12i32; }
    }
    // 3. an owner MOVED through the trait-qualified spelling into a `move Self:self`
    {
        SS:w = SS{ s: string_concat("ab", "c"), k: 1i64 };
        int64:n = 5i64;
        {
            drop Tm.mm(move(w), move(n), done);
        }
        int64:got3 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got3 != 9705i64) { exit 13i32; }
        string:after3 = string_concat("a", "b");
        if (after3.len != 2i64) { exit 13i32; }
    }
    // 4. (r3) a by-value plain-data receiver through an EXPLICIT dereference, `(<-pp).lookp()`: the pointee is
    //    copied at the spawn, and the owner's write under the task is not what the task reads
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        PD->:pp = @pd;
        {
            drop (<-pp).lookpd(done);
            pp.k = 2i64;
        }
        int64:got4 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got4 != 1i64) { exit 14i32; }
        if (pd.k != 2i64) { exit 14i32; }
    }
    // 5. (r7) the pointee copied into a local first, the owner writing through the pointer under the task
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        PD->:pp = @pd;
        PD:c = <-pp;
        {
            drop c.lookpd(done);
            pp.k = 3i64;
        }
        int64:got5 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got5 != 1i64) { exit 15i32; }
        if (pd.k != 3i64) { exit 15i32; }
    }
    // 6. a generic `T` MOVED into a `move T:x` parameter, at `string`
    {
        await generic_moved::<string>(string_concat("ab", "c")) ?| NIL;
        string:after = string_concat("d", "e");
        if (after.len != 2i64) { exit 16i32; }
    }
    // 7. a pick expression as the receiver, its arm viewing `d` and writing ANOTHER string
    {
        string:d = string_concat("hello", " world");
        string:o = string_concat("ab", "c");
        {
            drop (pick (1i32) { (*) {
                fixed uint8[]:v = string_bytes(d);
                o = string_concat("xyzzy", "!");
                give W{ k: (v[0i64] => int64) }; } }).lookd(done);
        }
        int64:got7 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got7 != 104i64) { exit 17i32; }
        if (o.len != 6i64) { exit 17i32; }
    }
    // 8. a pick expression as the receiver, the root written AFTER the block's join
    {
        string:d = string_concat("hello", " world");
        {
            drop (pick (1i32) { (*) {
                fixed uint8[]:v = string_bytes(d);
                give W{ k: (v[0i64] => int64) }; } }).lookd(done);
        }
        d = string_concat("xyzzy", "!");
        int64:got8 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got8 != 104i64) { exit 18i32; }
        if (d.len != 6i64) { exit 18i32; }
    }
    // 9. a pick expression inside a sanctioned argument, its arm viewing `d` and writing another string
    {
        shared_arena<int64>:s = shared_arena_make(8i64);
        shared_arena<int64>->:sp = @s;
        string:d = string_concat("hello", " world");
        string:o = string_concat("ab", "c");
        {
            drop fillerd(pick (1i32) { (*) {
                fixed uint8[]:v = string_bytes(d);
                o = string_concat("xyzzy", "!");
                if (v[0i64] != 104u8) { o = string_concat("bad", "!"); }
                give sp; } }, done);
        }
        int64:got9 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got9 != 424242i64) { exit 19i32; }
        if (o.len != 6i64) { exit 19i32; }
        s.destroy();
    }
    // 10. (r11b) the by-value method AWAITED through the EXPLICIT dereference, `(<-pp).peekk()`: the pointee is copied
    //     into the child frame and the task reports the value it read. (The auto-dereferenced `await pp.peekk()` is
    //     DEF-363's AWAIT face: the frame is built from the POINTER's words, `self.k` an address, 114 at both legs on
    //     168 and 169 -- r10b; the first cut of this control, `await pp.lookp() ?| NIL`, swallowed that failure and
    //     tested nothing.)
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        PD->:pp = @pd;
        int64:got = await (<-pp).peekk() ?! E1;
        if (got != 1i64) { exit 20i32; }
    }
    // 21. (r1; landing 169's refusal site 14) plain data through a pointer local, `drop pp.lookp()`, the owner
    //     writing under the task: the seed copies the POINTEE into the task's frame (DEF-363), so the task reads
    //     1, never the write and never the pointer's words -- and reports it (landing 171, test item C: until then
    //     `lookp` checked inside the task, a failure exiting 114 by the join's relay, not 21)
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        PD->:pp = @pd;
        {
            drop pp.lookpd(done);
            pp.k = 2i64;
        }
        int64:got21 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got21 != 1i64) { exit 21i32; }
        if (pd.k != 2i64) { exit 21i32; }
    }
    // 22. (r8; site 15) the same with nothing written
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        PD->:pp = @pd;
        {
            drop pp.lookpd(done);
        }
        int64:got22 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got22 != 1i64) { exit 22i32; }
        if (pd.k != 1i64) { exit 22i32; }
    }
    // 23. (r2; site 16) through an inline address, `drop (@pd).lookp()`, the owner writing under the task
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        {
            drop (@pd).lookpd(done);
            pd.k = 3i64;
        }
        int64:got23 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got23 != 1i64) { exit 23i32; }
        if (pd.k != 3i64) { exit 23i32; }
    }
    // 24. (r10b) the by-value method AWAITED through the AUTO-DEREFERENCE, `await pp.peekk()`, reporting the value
    //     it read: 1 (114 at both legs on 168 and 169, the pointer's words read as the struct -- DEF-363)
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        PD->:pp = @pd;
        int64:got = await pp.peekk() ?! E1;
        if (got != 1i64) { exit 24i32; }
    }
    // 25. (r12) a plain `async` by-value method SPAWNED through a pointer, `drop pp.lookqd(done)`, nothing written
    {
        PD:pd = PD{ k: 1i64 };
        PD->:pp = @pd;
        {
            drop pp.lookqd(done);
        }
        int64:got25 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got25 != 1i64) { exit 25i32; }
        if (pd.k != 1i64) { exit 25i32; }
    }
    // 26. an OWNING pointee AWAITED through a pointer by a by-value (not `move`) receiver, `await sp.slen()`: the
    //     child reads its loan of the header while the caller is suspended (D-177) -- 3, where 168 and 169 read the
    //     pointer's words as a `string` header (a spawn of it stays refused: sites 4-6 of the rejection file)
    {
        SS:ss = SS{ s: string_concat("ab", "c"), k: 5i64 };
        SS->:sp = @ss;
        int64:n = await sp.slen() ?! E1;
        if (n != 3i64) { exit 26i32; }
    }
    // 27. (x4) a pointee whose type HOLDS AN ADDRESS, `H{ p: @d }`, AWAITED through a pointer by a by-value receiver,
    //     `await hp.rdb()`: the caller is suspended until the child ends (D-177), so nothing writes what the copy's
    //     address names while the child reads it -- 104, the first byte of `d`, which is rewritten AFTER the await
    //     (168 and 169 read the pointer's words as `H`: x4, `rd` failing inside, 114 at O0 and 109 at O2)
    {
        string:d = string_concat("hello", " world");
        H:h = H{ p: @d };
        H->:hp = @h;
        uint8:b = await hp.rdb() ?! E1;
        d = string_concat("xyzzy", "!");
        if (b != 104u8) { exit 27i32; }
    }
    // 28. the owning pointee TRANSFERRED into a `move` receiver through the pointer, `(move(<-sp)).takec()` -- the
    //     transfer the spawn rule's owner message names, in a function that hands no borrow to a call; the pointee is
    //     left VACANT and assigned before a read (landing 172, F4: the task REPORTS the first byte it read, 97, as
    //     `takecd`). In a function of its own, `pointee_moved_recv`, since R1's commit: see the note above it
    {
        int64:bad28 = await pointee_moved_recv(done) ?! E1;
        int64:got28 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got28 != 97i64) { exit 28i32; }
        if (bad28 != 0i64) { exit 28i32; }
    }
    // 29. a CLONE of the owning pointee into a `move` receiver, `((<-sp).clone() ?! E1).takec()` -- the second
    //     transfer the message names (`.clone()` is fallible); the owner writes the pointee under the task, and
    //     the task reports the first BYTE it read -- 97, the clone's, never the owner's rewrite (landing 171, test
    //     item C: until then the control compared the LENGTH, 3 for both strings)
    {
        SC:w = SC{ s: string_concat("ab", "c"), k: 1i64 };
        SC->:sp = @w;
        {
            drop ((<-sp).clone() ?! E1).takecd(done);
            sp.s = string_concat("zz", "z");
        }
        int64:got29 = await done.recv(raw duration_ms(2000i64)) ?! E1;
        if (got29 != 97i64) { exit 29i32; }
        fixed uint8[]:wb = string_bytes(w.s);
        if (wb[0i64] != 122u8) { exit 29i32; }
    }
    // 30. DEF-364's remedy: the task is handed the VALUE it reads, never the address -- a plain struct built from
